A||N||5||ur2biteme||getting spark, not getting fuel on warm startup......||||1||
Z||000000||ur2biteme||02-18-2001||06:30 AM||||can anyone shed any light on this for me?  i've been searching the archives and cant find anything along these lines.  (WARNING: Long Post)<P>here's the deal.  i have an '87 4cyl with 171k on it.  the car sat for 4 months without a fuel stabalizer.  i know better, but i did it anyway.  i ran a couple of tanks through the car after it sat with no problems.  then i had a completely blocked fuel filter.  replaced that.  the car stalled out on me again.  i figured i had varnish in the line from the gas sitting that got broke up by the new gas.  i changed the fuel filter again.  still wouldn't start.  came back the next morning (about 9 hours later) fired right up, drove to work.  tried to start it up 6 hours later to drive home, wouldnt start.  came back about a day later (about 27-28 hours later) fired right up, drove it home, idled and allowed me to rev it as usual.  turned it off.  tried to start it an hour later, no-go.  started right up the next morning (about 9 hours later)<P>to make a long story short, i've got some additives in there that are supposed to be good about preventing varnish buildup (some stuff called outlaw.  i think pennzoil makes it) its helped some, but i still have to wait like 3-4 hours after i shut the car off to start it back up again.  in fact, i just drove around town for 2 hours just to see if it would die on me.  ran good, a bit of a preignition pinging that i never noticed, but was o.k., tried to start it 2 hours later, nothing. the car turns over, gets spark, but nothing comes out of the injector.  i can hear the fuel pump go through it's 2 second cycle when i turn the key, it's not loud and never has shown any signs of burning out, so i dont think it's the problem.  no blown fuses, etc.  <P>i'm thinking i've got a clog somewere in the fuel system that has to float down in the tank before the car will start, and once the car is started, there is enough fuel pressure to push around it, but not enough pressure to push around it on startup.  i dont know if that sounds farfetched or not, i'm just looking for suggestions.  i'm not fond of walking or pushing.  <P>thanks for the input.  <P>------------------<BR>Bob's '87 econo coupe<BR>aproaching 171k :)<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/ur2biteme" TARGET=_blank>a little piece of my madness</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||02-18-2001||07:11 AM||||You can try to flush the fuel system.<BR>*Note* make sure to catch the fuel and disable ignition system.<BR>1) Disconnect fuel inlet at filter, cycle pump. Reconnect line.<BR>2) Disconnect fuel supply at throttle body, cycle pump.<BR>3) Remove injector, clean screen with LOW pressure compressed air, make sure there are no debriesleft around inside throttle body inlet and around the injector sleeve. <BR>4) Reconnect fuel line, cover throttle body and cycle pump.<BR>5) Reinstall injector and test run to see if problem reoccures.<BR>You may need to wait a while to start as step 4 may flood engine if too much fuel runs through system.<BR>Don't forget to reconnect ignition!<BR>Hope this helps!<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 02-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||02-19-2001||08:02 PM||||When it doesn't start.... You have actually looked at the injector and seen that it's not dropping fuel?<P>You can try to clean the injector screen. It might help. Just as likely to wreck the screen. Even a tiny amount of damage to the screen is unacceptable.<P>Could be the fuel pressure regulator has crap in it causing low fuel pressure.<P>At the least I'd put a rebuild kit in it. It has all the rubbers and gaskets plus a new fuel regulator diaphragm and a new scerrn for the injector. It sounds allot like the injector probably has crap inside it that is binding it when it's hot.<P>Before you repace the injector. buy a "noid" light. Get the car hot and plug the light into the injector connector and turn the key while someone watches the light. This will tell you if the ECM is trying to fire the thing. If the light flashes then the injectors is probably gone. <P>You could try soaking the injector in staight Gumout INJECTOR cleaner. It might soak out. DO NOT use carb cleaner. Do Not soak the top of the injector. Only the part from the fuel openings on the sides down whould soak.||||reg||1
Z||000003||862M4inCA||02-19-2001||09:06 PM||||Mine did something similar a few months back, but the car wasn't sitting for any length of time (was a daily driver) and I would get spark, but no fuel, and I could hear the fuel pump buzzing in the tank. I solved the problem (most likely by overkill heh) by replacing the fuel pump and screen, as well as the injector. I probably could have gotten away with just replacing the pump (it was the original) but decided that with the age of the car and miles it could have had (odometer has been rolled UP to read about 300K!) that it was just a precaution. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ur2biteme||02-20-2001||12:07 AM||||well, youre right on about the injector ogre.  i checked it in a manner that is probably not the best.  while there was some pressure still on the line, but the injector not firing, i pulled the injector out.  the gas is flowing to the injector just fine (i have a gift for understatement) but the injector is not letting anything pass.  i'm gonna see about getting another one soon.  maybe it's been getting ready to clog like this for a while, considering my idle has been inconsistent for a while.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ur2biteme||02-21-2001||06:32 AM||||well, to finish the story, i went to autozone and bought a new injector.  less than 5 minutes of tinkering and it was changed.  turned the key and i'm back in business.  now i can start it anytime i want.  thanks.  ||||reg||1
